n" "q-341050" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341050" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341050" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{7{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{7{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341050" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{s}^{2}+49}{({s}^{2}-49)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\n \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t$ and $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms,\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}={{field-4}},\\,s>0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{tf(t)}=-{F}^{′}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =4,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}+16)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}=-{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1