{s}{{s}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{16s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{s}^{2}-4}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-340899" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>\\omega .\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)=-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6t,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>6.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)}={{field-4}},\\,s>6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)=-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6t.\n$$\n\nUsing the given result with $\\omega =6,$ the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6t$ is $\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36},$ so\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36},\\,s>6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(6({s}^{2}-36)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(-6({s}^{2}-36)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{12s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{12s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{(s)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}-36)^{2}-s\\cdot {(({s}^{2}-36)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}-36)^{4}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}-s\\cdot 2({s}^{2}-36)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{4}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-36)^{2}-4{s}^{2}({s}^{2}-36)}{({s}^{2}-36)^{4}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{{s}^{2}-36-4{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{-3{s}^{2}-36}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find ${F}^{″}(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-6t)}={F}^{″}(s)=-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}},\\,s>6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{F}^{″}(s)"] ["math" "-{s}^{2}{F}^{′}(s)"] ["math" "-{s}^{2}{F}^{″}(s)"] ["math" "{F}^{″}(s)"] ["math" "{s}^{2}{F}^{″}(s)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{F}^{″}(s)"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-36}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-36}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{18{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{18{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{36{s}^{3}+432s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{36{s}^{3}+432s}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341222" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{36{s}^{2}+432}{({s}^{2}-36)^{3}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>\\omega .\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4t$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>4.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4t}={{field-4}},\\,s>4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =4,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16},\\,s>4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}-16)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}-16)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}-16)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{{s}^{2}+16}{({s}^{2}-16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{({s}^{2}+16)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}-16)^{2}-({s}^{2}+16)\\cdot {(({s}^{2}-16)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}-16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{(2s)({s}^{2}-16)^{2}-({s}^{2}+16)\\cdot 2({s}^{2}-16)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2s({s}^{2}-16)^{2}-4s({s}^{2}+16)({s}^{2}-16)}{({s}^{2}-16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2s({s}^{2}-16)-4s({s}^{2}+16)}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2{s}^{3}-32s-4{s}^{3}-64s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find ${F}^{″}(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4t}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}},\\,s>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{F}^{″}(s)"] ["math" "-{s}^{2}{F}^{′}(s)"] ["math" "-{s}^{2}{F}^{″}(s)"] ["math" "{F}^{″}(s)"] ["math" "{s}^{2}{F}^{″}(s)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{F}^{″}(s)"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}-16}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2{s}^{3}+32s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{s}^{3}+32s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2{s}^{2}+96}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{s}^{2}+96}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-341216" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2{s}^{3}+96s}{({s}^{2}-16)^{3}}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=-3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{-3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =L{-3t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3L{t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =-3L{t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s\\cdot {s}^{2}-3({s}^{2}+1)}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{3}-3{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-331645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331645" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{s^2}+\\frac{s}{s^2+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-89595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t$ for $s>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-89595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =L{3}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =3L{1}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({s}^{2}+9)-{s}^{2}}{s({s}^{2}+9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}+27-{s}^{2}}{s({s}^{2}+9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-89595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-89595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-89595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2{s}^{2}+15}{s(s+9)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2{s}^{2}+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)}"] ["math" "\\frac{3{s}^{2}+1}{s(s+9)^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{4s+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{s({s}^{2}+9)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-89595" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2{s}^{2}+27}{s({s}^{2}+9)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=(1-5t)^{2}$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t)=(1-5t)^{2}=1-10t+25{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1-10t+25{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =L{1}+L{-10t}+L{25{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =L{1}-10L{t}+25L{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1}-10L{t}+25L{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-10\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+25\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{50}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-10s+50}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-330949" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330949" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{10}{s^2}+\\frac{50}{s^3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=(4t+1)^{2}$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t)=(4t+1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}+8t+1\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{16{t}^{2}+8t+1} \\\\  & =L{16{t}^{2}}+L{8t}+L{1} \\\\  & =16L{{t}^{2}}+8L{t}+L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =16L{{t}^{2}}+8L{t}+L{1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+8s+32}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-330950" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-330950" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{8}{s^2}+\\frac{32}{s^3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={t}^{3}-4t+5$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}-4t+5} & =L{{t}^{3}}+L{-4t}+L{5} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{3}}-4L{t}+5L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{3}}-4L{t}+5L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}-\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{s}^{3}-4{s}^{2}+6}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-331109" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-331109" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{s^4}-\\frac{4}{s^2}+\\frac{5}{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then $F(s)$ is twice differentiable for all $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$  \n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "the definition of the Laplace transform"] ["text" "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integrating by parts with respect to $s$"] ["text" "the continuity of partial derivatives"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "differentiating under the integral sign"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign again"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "substitution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334879" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "substitution"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1}.$ Since $f\\text{ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous}$, \nthere exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges. This justifies the expression in line $\\text{L1}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}F(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\ge 0"] ["text" "$f$ is bounded on $[0,\\infty )$"] ["text" "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous"] ["text" "${e}^{-st}$ is infinitely differentiable"] ["text" "the Laplace transform is linear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "the definition of the Laplace transform"] ["text" "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "the continuity of partial derivatives"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334832" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "differentiating under the integral sign"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ and\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}F(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-t{e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L6}.$ By $\\text{the definition of the Laplace transform},$\n\n$$\nL{t\\cdot f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}(tf(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the integral in line $\\text{L5}$, we see that\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}(tf(t))\\,dt=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)},\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the expression in line $\\text{L6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "the definition of the Laplace transform"] ["text" "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the definition of the derivative with respect to $s$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign again"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "substitution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "substitution"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the chain rule"] ["text" "the definition of the Laplace transform"] ["text" "the product rule"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334829" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the definition of the Laplace transform"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1}.$ Since $f\\text{ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous}$, \nthere exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges. This justifies the expression in line $\\text{L1}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\ge 0"] ["text" "$f$ is of exponential order"] ["text" "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous"] ["text" "${e}^{-st}$ is infinitely differentiable"] ["text" "the Laplace transform is linear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integrating by parts with respect to $s$"] ["text" "the continuity of partial derivatives"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "differentiating under the integral sign"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign again"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "substitution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334876" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "substitution"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise continuous function on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ converges for every $s>a.$\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a,$\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous, there exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The standard existence theorem for the Laplace transform says that if $f$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and of exponential order $a,$ then the improper integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges for every $s>a.$ This is exactly what it means for $F(s)$ to exist for all $s>a.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The smoothness theorem guarantees\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}\n$$\n\nfor every $n\\ge 0$ and each $s>a,$ which shows that $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable, even if $f(t)$ itself is not.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7d7021b2455a038b7b1bf4159e03b9bd1be4c71d3cbd85f476d8dc9cbff0f05b"} {"question" "q-334881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "q-334881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/state.json" "file_sha256" "87a8916b8d9846e6f14d0436da220894fa55939a810807f1667b98ea77147515"} {"question" "e-21834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t \\cos 5t\\}.\n$$\n\n \nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{\\cos \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-21834.json" "file_sha256" "9bc264dc2685d27aaebbb1f9019fcd79b9902a10ff6962ab5c3416ddb24c81a0"} {"question" "e-21834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ tf(t) \\} = -F'(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = \\cos 5t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega = 5,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+25},\n\\qquad s > 0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s({s}^{2}+25)^{-1}) \\\\  & =({s}^{2}+25)^{-1}+s(-({s}^{2}+25)^{-2}\\cdot 2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+25}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}}-\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}=-{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}-25}{({s}^{2}+25)^{2}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-21834.json" "file_sha256" "9bc264dc2685d27aaebbb1f9019fcd79b9902a10ff6962ab5c3416ddb24c81a0"} {"question" "e-21838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^2 \\sinh{5t}\\}.\n$$\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{\\sinh \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2-\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > \\omega.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-21838.json" "file_sha256" "60f32ea4619102f75267c2af74e718b02aed3ba722beadf3c2e5f83a7112fafa"} {"question" "e-21838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2 f(t) \\} = F''(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = \\sinh 5t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega = 5,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{5}{s^2-25},\n\\qquad s > 5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(5({s}^{2}-25)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-5({s}^{2}-25)^{-2}\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-\\frac{10s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{10s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{(s)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-s\\cdot {(({s}^{2}-25)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-s\\cdot 2({s}^{2}-25)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}-25)^{2}-4{s}^{2}({s}^{2}-25)}{({s}^{2}-25)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{{s}^{2}-25-4{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-10\\cdot (\\frac{-3{s}^{2}-25}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30{s}^{2}+250}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find $F''(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5t}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{30{s}^{2}+250}{({s}^{2}-25)^{3}},\\,s>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-21838.json" "file_sha256" "60f32ea4619102f75267c2af74e718b02aed3ba722beadf3c2e5f83a7112fafa"} {"question" "e-6643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = (t - 5)^2$ for $s > 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-6643.json" "file_sha256" "5558fb8f65cbb07188e4a5a8f9074971b71f6c2f54b90f2998c439ed8119e396"} {"question" "e-6643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t) = (t - 5)^2= t^2 - 10t + 25\n$$\n\nNow, taking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{2}-10t+25} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+L{-10t}+L{25} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}-10L{t}+25L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{t}^{2}}-10L{t}+25L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-10\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+25\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{25}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-10s+25{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{s}^{2}-10s+2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-6643.json" "file_sha256" "5558fb8f65cbb07188e4a5a8f9074971b71f6c2f54b90f2998c439ed8119e396"} {"question" "e-21646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for $s > a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a,$ and we have\n\n$$\nF''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{ t^2\\cdot f(t) \\}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$ exists for $s > a.$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2}{\\textrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$\n\nExplain the reasoning behind:\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}.$\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from its preceding line.\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-21646.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd65c6162faa25f7b90c08f66fa69815ee35020edf696b99ec3b72b15b691e"} {"question" "e-21646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and $s > a,$ we have\n\n$$\nF^{(n)}(s) = (-1)^n\\, \\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n = 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}.$ From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nBy the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s,$ we get\n\n$$\nF''(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2} F(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\textrm{L2}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\textrm{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\n  = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}(e^{-st})\n  = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial s}(-te^{-st})\n  = f(t)\\,(t^2 e^{-st})\n  = t^2 e^{-st} f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the chain rule.\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L6}.$ By the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}\n  = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the integral in line $\\textrm{L5}$, we see that\n\n$$\nF''(s)\n  = \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n  = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n  = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\},\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the expression in line $\\textrm{L6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072103/example-21646.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd65c6162faa25f7b90c08f66fa69815ee35020edf696b99ec3b72b15b691e"}] "usage" {"e-21838" [] "e-21646" [] "e-6643" [] "e-21834" []} "basis" 2615 "retractions" [[17592186221136 :question/problem "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n$\\mathcal L\\{t \\cos 5t\\}$.\nYou may make use of the following result:\n$\\mathcal L \\{\\cos \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0$."] [17592186221136 :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty)]$ and has exponential order $a$, then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a$, and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer\n\n$$\nn\\geq 0\n$$\n\nand every such $s$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s)\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{tf(t) \\} = -F'(s)\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos 5t\n$$\n\nUsing the given result with $\\omega = 5$, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+25}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF^{′}(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}(\\frac{s}{s^{2} + 25}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{ds}(s(s^{2} + 25)^{-1}) \\\\\n&= (s^{2} + 25)^{-1} + s(- (s^{2} + 25)^{-2} \\cdot 2s) \\\\\n&= (s^{2} + 25)/((s^{2} + 25)^{2}) - (2s^{2})/((s^{2} + 25)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (25 - s^{2})/((s^{2} + 25)^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\nL{t\\cos 5t} =-F^{′}(s) = (s^{2} - 25)/((s^{2} + 25)^{2}), s > 0\n$$"] [17592186221140 :question/problem "Use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of\n$\\mathcal L\\{t^2 \\sinh{5t}\\}$.\nYou may make use of the following result:\n$\\mathcal L \\{\\sinh \\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2-\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > \\omega$."] [17592186221140 :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty)]$ and has exponential order $a$, then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a$, and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer\n\n$$\nn\\geq 0\n$$\n\nand every such $s$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s)\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2 f(t) \\} = F''(s)\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\sinh 5t\n$$\n\nUsing the given result with $\\omega = 5$, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{5}{s^2-25}, \\qquad s > 5\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF^{′}(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}(\\frac{5}{s^{2} - 25}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{ds}(5(s^{2} - 25)^{-1}) \\\\\n&=-5(s^{2} - 25)^{-2} \\cdot 2s \\\\\n&= - (10s)/((s^{2} - 25)^{2}) \\\\\nF^{″}(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}(- (10s)/((s^{2} - 25)^{2})) \\\\\n&=-10 \\cdot \\frac{d}{ds}((s)/((s^{2} - 25)^{2})) \\\\\n&=-10 \\cdot (((s)^{′} \\cdot (s^{2} - 25)^{2} - s \\cdot ((s^{2} - 25)^{2})^{′})/((s^{2} - 25)^{4})) \\\\\n&=-10 \\cdot (((s^{2} - 25)^{2} - s \\cdot 2(s^{2} - 25) \\cdot 2s)/((s^{2} - 25)^{4})) \\\\\n&=-10 \\cdot (((s^{2} - 25)^{2} - 4s^{2}(s^{2} - 25))/((s^{2} - 25)^{4})) \\\\\n&=-10 \\cdot ((s^{2} - 25 - 4s^{2})/((s^{2} - 25)^{3})) \\\\\n&=-10 \\cdot ((-3s^{2} - 25)/((s^{2} - 25)^{3})) \\\\\n&= (30s^{2} + 250)/((s^{2} - 25)^{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find $F''(s)$.\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\nL{t^{2}\\sinh 5t} = F^{″}(s) = (30s^{2} + 250)/((s^{2} - 25)^{3}), s > 5\n$$"] [17592186221150 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$, find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = (t - 5)^2$ for $s > 0$."] [17592186221150 :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t) = (t - 5)^2= t^2 - 10t + 25\n$$\n\nNow, taking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= L{t^{2} - 10t + 25} \\\\\n&= L{t^{2}} + L{-10t} + L{25} \\\\\n&= L{t^{2}} - 10L{t} + 25L{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{t^n\\right\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{t^{2}} &= \\frac{2}{s^{3}}, s > 0 \\\\\nL{t} &= \\frac{1}{s^{2}}, s > 0 \\\\\nL{1} &= \\frac{1}{s}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= L{t^{2}} - 10L{t} + 25L{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} - 10 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + 25 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{10}{s^{2}} + \\frac{25}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 10s + 25s^{2}}{s^{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25s^{2} - 10s + 2}{s^{3}}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186221109 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty)]$ and its Laplace transform\n$F(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}$\nexists for $s > a$. Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s > a$, and we have\n$F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t) \\}$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\:$ $F(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$ exists for $s > a$.\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2}{\\textrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\:$ $\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\:$ $F''(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$\n\nExplain the reasoning behind:\n\n- How line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$.\n- How line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from its preceding line.\n- How line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$."] [17592186221109 :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty)]$ and has exponential order $a$, then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a$, and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer\n\n$$\nn\\geq 0\n$$\n\nand $s > a$, we have\n\n$$\nF^{(n)}(s) = (-1)^n\\, \\mathcal{L}\\{t^n f(t) \\}\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n = 2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}$. From line $\\textrm{L1}$ we know that\n$F(s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\nBy the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$, we get\n$F'(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2} F(s) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2}{\\mathrm{d}s^2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$,\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\textrm{L2}$.\n- We now consider line $\\textrm{L4}$. The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n$\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}\\big(e^{-st} f(t)\\big) = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial s^2}(e^{-st}) = f(t)\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial s}(-te^{-st}) = f(t)\\,(t^2 e^{-st}) = t^2 e^{-st} f(t)$.\nThus line $\\textrm{L4}$ follows from the chain rule.\n- Finally, we consider line $\\textrm{L6}$. By the definition of the Laplace transform,\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\nComparing this with the integral in line $\\textrm{L5}$, we see that\n$F''(s) = \\int_0^\\infty t^2 e^{-st} f(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} \\big(t^2 f(t)\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^2\\cdot f(t)\\}$,\nwhich is exactly the expression in line $\\textrm{L6}$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
